KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ T is_constructible from S via operator T\(\): SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ S is_convertible to T via operator T\(\): SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG must FAIL: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [over.match.copy]/1: converting to class type T from class type S
considers both T's converting constructors AND S's conversion functions.  CBMC's
implicit_conversion_sequence / user_defined_conversion_sequence only enumerates
the target's constructors and ignores the source's conversion operators, so
is_constructible<T,S> and is_convertible<S,T> are wrongly false when only
S::operator T() realises the conversion (g++/clang++ agree both are true).
Header-free.  Flip to CORE once user_defined_conversion_sequence also considers
the source type's conversion functions.
